o smoke: a qualitative study</article-title><source>Br J Gen Pract</source><year>2000</year><volume>50</volume><fpage>207</fpage><lpage>210</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10750230</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chesterman</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Judge</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Bauld</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Ferguson</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>How effective are the English smoking treatment services in reaching disadvantaged smokers?</article-title><source>Addiction</source><year>2005</year><volume>100</volume><fpage>36</fpage><lpage>45</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15755261</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/j.1360-0443.2005.01026.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>McCormick</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Fleming</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Charlton</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><source>Morbidity Statistics from General Practice. Fourth national study 1991&#x02013;1992</source><year>1995</year><publisher-name>London, HMSO</publisher-name></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>